1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,818
Imagine a neighbor asks where they
can get help using a computer.

2
00:00:04,210 --> 00:00:06,283
You may already have a familiar library,

3
00:00:06,290 --> 00:00:08,984
community center,
or veterans' organization in mind.

4
00:00:09,530 --> 00:00:13,770
Use that experience as a starting point,
then verify what is available now.

5
00:00:14,590 --> 00:00:16,973
Our task is to find a
suitable public service,

6
00:00:17,610 --> 00:00:19,527
record enough evidence to revisit it,

7
00:00:19,710 --> 00:00:23,337
and organize a fictional handout
without exposing private information.

8
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:25,730
You can use a different local

9
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:27,050
service that matters to you.

10
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:30,230
The names and details in this
demonstration are examples,

11
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:33,053
not a claim about any
particular organization.

12
00:00:34,410 --> 00:00:35,470
A useful search ends

13
00:00:35,470 --> 00:00:38,838
with a supported decision,
not just a page that looks impressive.

14
00:00:40,545 --> 00:00:42,877
Start with the words
that describe your task,

15
00:00:43,205 --> 00:00:45,174
the kind of service, and the location.

16
00:00:46,005 --> 00:00:51,685
Computer help library plus your town is
more useful than typing help by itself.

17
00:00:52,925 --> 00:00:56,705
Inspect the result title and the
website address before opening it.

18
00:00:57,405 --> 00:01:01,185
If the results are too broad,
add a specific service or location.

19
00:01:02,325 --> 00:01:03,965
If there are no useful results,

20
00:01:04,505 --> 00:01:08,184
remove one restriction rather than
assuming the service does not exist.

21
00:01:09,685 --> 00:01:12,885
Filters can help,
but they can also hide a relevant page.

22
00:01:13,905 --> 00:01:16,885
Pause and write a search for
your chosen community task.

23
00:01:17,805 --> 00:01:19,618
Explain what each word contributes.

24
00:01:20,705 --> 00:01:25,205
Then change one term and compare the
results instead of opening every link.

25
00:01:26,956 --> 00:01:30,065
A page can be accurate and still
be wrong for your decision.

26
00:01:30,676 --> 00:01:34,676
An official library page from another
town may describe a real service,

27
00:01:35,256 --> 00:01:37,484
but not the one available
to your neighbor.

28
00:01:37,776 --> 00:01:39,538
Ask who published the information,

29
00:01:40,036 --> 00:01:41,296
what evidence supports it,

30
00:01:41,856 --> 00:01:46,216
when it was updated, why it was written,
and whether it answers your question.

31
00:01:47,116 --> 00:01:48,136
For opening hours

32
00:01:48,136 --> 00:01:50,416
or eligibility, go to the organization

33
00:01:50,416 --> 00:01:53,756
responsible for that service
and check the details there.

34
00:01:53,756 --> 00:01:57,296
Search-result snippets may be incomplete.

35
00:01:58,596 --> 00:02:00,254
Compare two pages that disagree.

36
00:02:01,276 --> 00:02:03,336
Do not count votes between websites;

37
00:02:04,436 --> 00:02:06,956
identify which source is
in a position to know,

38
00:02:06,956 --> 00:02:11,836
and confirm uncertain details through
an independently found contact method.

39
00:02:13,622 --> 00:02:17,197
Suppose you find a useful service
today and need it again next month.

40
00:02:17,942 --> 00:02:20,962
A note saying good website
will not be enough.

41
00:02:21,802 --> 00:02:26,542
Record the organization, the page title,
the web address, the date you checked it,

42
00:02:26,922 --> 00:02:30,702
and the particular fact you plan to use.
A bookmark helps you return,

43
00:02:31,222 --> 00:02:35,263
but it does not freeze the page or prove
that the information remains current.

44
00:02:36,502 --> 00:02:40,284
In your handout, distinguish a checked
fact from your own interpretation.

45
00:02:40,982 --> 00:02:45,302
If the page does not answer a question,
write needs confirmation

46
00:02:45,302 --> 00:02:50,502
rather than filling the gap with a guess.
This is the digital equivalent

47
00:02:50,502 --> 00:02:53,714
of writing the source beside a
number in a household notebook.

48
00:02:55,482 --> 00:02:57,503
An online form is a structured request.

49
00:02:58,122 --> 00:03:00,764
Read its purpose and required
fields before typing.

50
00:03:01,562 --> 00:03:02,877
In our course practice form,

51
00:03:03,042 --> 00:03:06,928
choose the kind of computer help needed
and a fictional contact preference.

52
00:03:07,202 --> 00:03:09,948
Review the entries before
selecting the final button.

53
00:03:10,722 --> 00:03:11,567
On a real service,

54
00:03:11,842 --> 00:03:14,795
ask whether a sensitive detail
is necessary for the task,

55
00:03:15,062 --> 00:03:17,083
and verify the site before entering it.

56
00:03:17,542 --> 00:03:19,882
Our practice does not send a real request.

57
00:03:20,862 --> 00:03:22,762
That distinction matters: seeing

58
00:03:22,762 --> 00:03:26,752
a confirmation message in a simulator
proves that you completed the exercise,

59
00:03:27,002 --> 00:03:29,230
not that an organization
received anything.

60
00:03:29,922 --> 00:03:31,142
Pause, complete

61
00:03:31,142 --> 00:03:34,821
the fictional form, and explain what
you deliberately left out and why.

62
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:39,620
Think of a folder as a labeled
place to put related work.

63
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:41,280
Create a practice

64
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:45,200
folder for the community resource
task and use a descriptive file name,

65
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:49,880
such as library-help-draft.

66
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:53,480
A file saved on this computer may
not appear on another computer.

67
00:03:54,340 --> 00:03:57,400
A cloud file belongs to
a service and an account,

68
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:01,000
and a synced folder may keep
copies in more than one place.

69
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:06,720
Before moving to another device, check the
account and the saving or syncing status.

70
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:10,328
Reopen the file from
the intended location.

71
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:13,376
That simple test catches a common mistake:

72
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:17,700
editing one copy while later opening
an older copy somewhere else.

73
00:04:19,527 --> 00:04:23,776
Our partner should suggest clearer wording
without rewriting the handout directly.

74
00:04:23,927 --> 00:04:25,907
Viewer access usually allows reading.

75
00:04:26,707 --> 00:04:30,334
Commenter access, when supported,
allows suggestions through comments.

76
00:04:31,367 --> 00:04:33,699
Editor access permits
changes to the content.

77
00:04:34,727 --> 00:04:37,587
Choose the smallest role
that fits the task, inspect

78
00:04:37,587 --> 00:04:41,027
the recipient carefully,
and have the partner verify that the file

79
00:04:41,027 --> 00:04:44,887
opens with the expected controls.
A link that works for you

80
00:04:44,887 --> 00:04:49,407
may still fail for someone else. Also
distinguish read-only from encryption.

81
00:04:49,407 --> 00:04:54,627
Read-only limits editing; it does not
necessarily prevent someone from reading

82
00:04:54,627 --> 00:04:59,547
or copying the information. Pause and
choose a role for someone who only

83
00:04:59,547 --> 00:05:04,027
needs to read the finished handout.
Viewer is the appropriate starting point.

84
00:05:05,775 --> 00:05:07,055
Sync is convenient because

85
00:05:07,055 --> 00:05:10,115
a change can travel between a
device and an online service.

86
00:05:10,935 --> 00:05:13,111
The same convenience can carry a deletion.

87
00:05:13,715 --> 00:05:17,475
Watch the practice file disappear
from one location and then the other.

88
00:05:18,415 --> 00:05:21,295
That is why sync alone
is not a separate backup.

89
00:05:22,195 --> 00:05:26,115
A backup provides another recovery
copy under an appropriate plan.

90
00:05:26,915 --> 00:05:29,255
Version history may
preserve earlier content,

91
00:05:29,715 --> 00:05:33,435
while a recycle bin may retain a
deleted file for a limited time.

92
00:05:34,775 --> 00:05:37,314
Availability and retention
depend on the service.

93
00:05:37,575 --> 00:05:40,475
Do not test deletion with
your only important file.

94
00:05:41,595 --> 00:05:42,495
Use the fictional

95
00:05:42,495 --> 00:05:43,495
copy and explain

96
00:05:43,495 --> 00:05:47,640
which protection would help if the mistake
spread through every synced location.

97
00:05:49,469 --> 00:05:51,069
First identify the problem.

98
00:05:52,229 --> 00:05:54,509
If the whole file is
missing after deletion,

99
00:05:54,949 --> 00:05:57,281
inspect the appropriate
trash or recycle bin.

100
00:05:58,049 --> 00:06:01,929
If the file exists but its wording
changed, inspect version history.

101
00:06:03,049 --> 00:06:07,298
Preview the earlier version and coordinate
with collaborators before restoring it.

102
00:06:08,109 --> 00:06:11,114
You do not want to replace
useful work someone just added.

103
00:06:12,169 --> 00:06:13,629
Compression is a separate tool:

104
00:06:14,409 --> 00:06:18,349
a ZIP file can package several files
for transfer and may reduce their size.

105
00:06:19,149 --> 00:06:22,169
It is not automatically
encryption or a backup plan.

106
00:06:23,229 --> 00:06:28,089
After extracting a practice ZIP, open a
file to confirm the contents are usable.

107
00:06:29,149 --> 00:06:33,190
Choose the tool that matches the problem
rather than trusting a familiar icon.

108
00:06:34,765 --> 00:06:38,985
Pause and build a small resource
folder for your chosen community task.

109
00:06:39,585 --> 00:06:44,065
Include a source note, a fictional
handout, and a clear file name.

110
00:06:44,845 --> 00:06:46,866
Tell a partner which fact you verified,

111
00:06:47,565 --> 00:06:51,825
why that source is appropriate,
and which permission fits the review task.

112
00:06:52,825 --> 00:06:53,925
Then explain how

113
00:06:53,925 --> 00:06:56,265
you would recover a deleted file and how

114
00:06:56,265 --> 00:06:58,545
that differs from
restoring earlier wording.

115
00:06:59,205 --> 00:07:01,825
If an account or sharing
feature is unavailable,

116
00:07:02,305 --> 00:07:06,265
demonstrate the decision with the
course simulator and label it simulated.

117
00:07:07,745 --> 00:07:10,705
For your next independent task,
reuse this sequence:

118
00:07:11,705 --> 00:07:12,785
ask a focused question,

119
00:07:13,765 --> 00:07:20,585
check the evidence, record a trail,
organize the file, and verify access.
